course: dictionary
lesson: 13302
title: One word, two meanings: graver
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Graver is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits.
explain: Grave means serious, so graver means more serious or more worrying. «The doctor's news was graver than we expected.» The news was more serious than expected, so graver is the adjective here.
explain: Graver can also be a noun, a sharp tool for cutting lines into metal, wood or stone. «She used a graver to cut her name into the silver spoon.» Here a graver is a thing you hold, not a feeling.
explain: Ask yourself: does "more serious" fit? Then it is the adjective. Could you say "a tool" instead? Then it is the noun.
fact: Graver is the comparing form of the adjective grave: grave, graver, gravest.
fact: «A graver danger» means a more serious danger.
fact: A graver, as a noun, is a sharp tool for cutting lines into metal, wood or stone.
fact: Engraving means making a picture or words by cutting lines into a hard surface, often with a graver.
fact: The same spelling can mean two different things, so always check the sentence around the word.
exercise: «The news from the hospital was graver than we thought.» What does graver mean here? | Smaller | *More serious | Quieter | Happier
exercise: Mia used a graver to cut a pattern into the metal plate. What is a graver? | A loud noise from a machine | A type of cake | A kind of bird that sings at night | *A sharp tool for cutting lines
exercise: Which word is the comparing form of grave? | gravel | *graver | grate | grave
test: «The storm brought a graver danger: the bridge was starting to crack.» What does graver mean? | Brighter | Less serious | Colder | *More serious
test: Ali's pattern was made with a graver on a silver plate. What is a graver used for? | *Cutting lines into metal, wood or stone | Painting walls | Cooking food | Measuring rain
exam: Which sentence uses graver as a tool? | The graver news made everyone quiet | The problem is graver than before | His mood grew graver as the day went on | *She cut a fine line in the wood with a graver
